</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span> system comprises three key components: speculative prefetcher (SP), priority-based cache policy (LCP), and cache configuration algorithm (CCA).
In this section, we conduct a set of experiments with Mixtral-8x7B to evaluate the effect of these three components.
For <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span> without SP, experts are prefetched randomly within each layer’s computation window.
In <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span> without LCP, the LCP policy is replaced by a random (RND) cache replacement strategy.
As for <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span> without CCA, we employ uniform VRAM budget allocation across layers and fix the expert split ratio at 0.5.
The results in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3. Memory Efficiency ‣ 4. Performance Evaluation ‣ Accelerating Mixture-of-Expert Inference with Adaptive Expert Split Mechanism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> demonstrate that CCA plays a crucial role in both the prefill and decode stages.
Specifically, without CCA, the TTFT and TPOT of <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span> are prolonged by about 1.21<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> and 1.46<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Although the SP and LCP components positively contribute to <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span> ’s performance, their impact is relatively modest, especially in the prefill stage.
These results indicate the necessity and importance of all three components, while CCA plays the most
critical role in <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span> ’s performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Sensitivity Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we conduct a set of experiments using Mixtral-8x7B to measure <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span>’s performance (<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, TPOT) with different settings of hyper-parameters, including the balance control parameter <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and observation window <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> of the LCP policy, as well as the allocation granularity <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> of the cache configuration algorithm.
Firstly, different settings of <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> only affect the cache hit rate slightly, without causing significant impacts to the inference latency.
Specifically, we present the TPOT of <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span> under five different settings of <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S4.F9.sf1" title="In Figure 9 ‣ 4.5. Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 4. Performance Evaluation ‣ Accelerating Mixture-of-Expert Inference with Adaptive Expert Split Mechanism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(a)</span></a>, where the latency variation is at most 8ms.
Secondly, a smaller granularity <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> allows for more flexible VRAM budget allocation, enabling better inference performance.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F9.sf2" title="In Figure 9 ‣ 4.5. Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 4. Performance Evaluation ‣ Accelerating Mixture-of-Expert Inference with Adaptive Expert Split Mechanism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(b)</span></a>, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span>’s TPOT increases as the granularity <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> becomes larger.
For instance, when <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.05, the latency is prolonged by about 12.12% compared to the default setting (<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>).
However, while a finer allocation granularity may yield performance gains, it also increases the number of iterations required for the algorithm to converge.
Overall, the performance of <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">MoEpic</span> exhibits minimal sensitivity to variations in hyper-parameter settings, underscoring its strong robustness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
